window.docContents[239].resolve({"/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--Write-Once___-Correctly--The-four-steps--Step-1-___-Start-from-the-measurement-principle___-declare-the-kinds":{"contents":"Before any number, name what each quantity is by the examination that produces it. A\nkind-of-property is the common defining aspect of mutually comparable\nproperties (Dybkær §6.19); two otherwise-comparable kinds are individuated by their\nexamination principle — the defining aspect of the procedure that\nmeasures them, so that a different principle forces a different\nkind. This is the layer a description logic partly has: it can record a class, but not the\noperator algebra or the laws the later steps add.In the example this is the catalogue of bare kinds — the radar backscatter , the\noptical ndvi, the surface reflectivity, and the Mironov dielectric fit-constant kinds — each\ndeclared as a def of KindOfProperty, named by the instrument or principle that examines it.\nThat a different examination principle forces a different kind is itself checked there: monostatic\nand bistatic reflectivity share name, scale, and dimension yet are distinct kinds. A kind is\ngeneral (a type); this pixel's backscatter is individual (a term, Quantity backscatter α).\nFix the kinds first: everything above is indexed by them.\n\n","context":"PropertyKindCalculus Blueprint\u0009Write Once, Correctly\u0009The four steps","header":"13.1.1. Step 1 — Start from the measurement principle: declare the kinds","id":"/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--Write-Once___-Correctly--The-four-steps--Step-1-___-Start-from-the-measurement-principle___-declare-the-kinds"}});